ull  downs press ups maybe these exercises  are simple so you can't injure yourself  by using poor movement patterns very  easily anyways and by using those high  rep ranges were doing a few different  interesting things first of all we're  plugging the muscles with blood and  metabolites causing metabolic stress and  there's a lead to growth and that's  important because if you want to stick  to a training program you want to see  progress early on this will help with  your adherence at the same time all that  blood flow all of that pump work is  going to help to fuel the tendons and  encourage them to grow stronger and  recover thicker and more powerful this  is important because muscle grows  stronger quicker than does tendon so you  could expect to see structural changes  in your muscle after just eight days of  training but you won't see the same  thing happening your tendons until two  months so a common issue that leads to  injury is if someone gets really  strongly quick and then they pile a lot  of weight on and then they can lift it  but their tendons give out so by doing  this pump work by doing high repetition  low weight for about two months before  you get into anything more complicated  you can build yourself that foundation  get strong enough get stronger tendons  so you don't cause yourself an injury  later on as you get more advanced next  we're gonna look at mobility so I  mentioned in the introduction that one  of the big problems a lot of people have  with their training when they're  starting out is that they lack the  mobility to perform the movements they  need to perform there's a difference  between mobility and flexibility so  flexibility is the ability to get into  any position in any way you like so you  can use an external force when you're  pushing against the wall using advanced  mobility on the other hand is your  ability to get into that position  without any external assistant  flexibility and mobility are both  important and of course they'll help  each other with so many people spending  a long amounts of time sitting it's  really common for mobility to be really  poor when you're typing at a computer  you're shortening your hip flexors  you're lengthening and weakening your  glutes  you've got rounded shoulders coming  forwards  what happens is the muscles that are  being shortened in this position you're  holding for hours and hours on end get  tighter and that becomes their resting  position and consider that a lot of  people can't even get into a deep squat  without going up on their tiptoes so how  can you train like a Batman how can you  squat with a heavy weight when you can't  even get into a deep squat so we need to  fix that fortunately there are a lot of  things we can do in order to improve our  mobility and our flexibility as we start  our training journey so like I say  there's crossover between flexibility  mobility so that's one of the places to  start improve your flexibility you can  check out the video that Grant made for  the channel but basically this is going  to involve holding static stretches for  about 30 seconds that tends to be the  recommended amount of time following a  workout these don't go at the start of  the workout they go after you've lifted  the weights your body will fight you as  it gets into those positions as it's  trying to prevent you from injuring  yourself so you want to convince your  body that it's fine you need to relax  try and relax the muscles and get into a  state of calm get comfortable in that  stretched position so don't go too far  just go far enough and you feel the  stretch and then relax there hang out  there this is recommended by Pavel Tatsu  'ln author of relaxin to stret but you  should also be focusing on mobility  itself and this can go into your  workouts and the way I recommend doing  that in particular is by using very  light weights and then performing  movements like the squat like the  deadlift and watching yourself as 